 1%2%3%4%5%  replay: {"kept":3,"found":"c43adfd4-8323-4502-8ed9-fc1937dad6ff"}
 
 
   Two identities travelled with the same aggregate, and only one survived:
 
                         live                                    replay                                  
     assembler's key     subscription-2                          subscription-2                          stable
     domain's identity   06240f53-9e59-4b63-a842-46aa39560dd3    c43adfd4-8323-4502-8ed9-fc1937dad6ff    not stable
 
   The lookup by the assembler's key succeeded on both sides - that is what
   'kept:3, found:...' reports twice. So the index the assembler authored is
   reproduced by replay, and the key the domain minted for itself is not.
   The call site that mints it is marked R_99 in SubscriptionPaymentBridge.
 
   The count survived replay. The identity did not, and the reason is
   inside the domain: SubscriptionPayment.Buy mints its own key with
   Guid.NewGuid() as it builds the aggregate.
 
   Replay re-runs the acts, so an act carrying a decision that cannot be
   repeated is not repeatable in that respect. Non-determinism is captured
   at the parameter plane - @Now is journaled and re-injected, an Eval is
   captured rather than re-executed - and a Guid drawn inside a domain
   factory is past that edge. Nothing here is a defect of either party:
   the domain was written to be called once, by a caller that would store
   what came back.
 
   What it costs the assembler is worth stating plainly: it inherits a
   constraint it did not author. A repertoire can be complete, executable
   and testable - and still not be replayable. Those are three properties,
   not one.


